window.docContents[40].resolve({"/Dedicated-kinds-of-property/The-dedicated-kind-_LPAR_Dybk___r-Ch___-20_RPAR_/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Dedicated-kinds-of-property--The-dedicated-kind-_LPAR_Dybk___r-Ch___-20_RPAR_":{"contents":"\n\nA component (§20) is the pertinent component of the sort of system a dedicated kind is about — water in a soil sample, glucose in plasma. Specified abstractly by identity, exactly mirroring the sort carrier.\n\nA one-field structure with DecidableEq; nothing to prove.\n\nA dedicated kind-of-property (§20) bundles a sort of system, a component, and a kind-of-property. Its systematicTerm renders the three as System — Component ; kind, and it is a dedicated kind-of-quantity iff its underlying kind has magnitude (§13.3.1). dedicatedTo builds one from a bare kind.\n\nA three-field structure with DecidableEq. systematicTerm is string interpolation of the three ids; IsQuantity is the underlying kind's IsQuantity.\n\nDedicating a kind through an object: the object contributes exactly its sort, via the model's Sorted claim. This is the arrow from the individual layer to the catalogue — an individual quantity carries the particular in its type, and dedicatedFor says which catalogue entry that particular's readings instantiate. In the terms of the foundations chapter's ontological square, it is Lowe's dispositional route to exemplification: up the instantiated by edge, then across the top.\n\ndedicatedTo applied to Sorted.sortOf o.\n\nTwo objects of one sort dedicate to one kind. The catalogue entry cannot tell two rovers apart, and is not supposed to: individuation is the object index's job, where combining quantities across the two is a compile-time type error. This is the load-bearing half of the corrected §20 reading — dedication to the sort, individuation by the object.\n\nCongruence: the two dedications differ only in the sort argument, and the hypothesis equates the sorts.\n\nSame sort and component, a different kind-of-property: a distinct dedicated kind. This is the principled volumetric-versus-gravimetric distinctness. Both water contents are \"Soil — Water\", but volume fraction and mass fraction are different kinds-of-property, so the dedicated kinds differ — with the sort and component held identical, and without appeal to any hand-chosen identity string. This is exactly the discrimination an OWL2 reasoner cannot phrase.\n\nAssume the two dedicated kinds are equal; then their kind projections are equal by congruence, contradicting the hypothesis (intro he; apply h; rw [he]). The companion laws distinct_of_sort and distinct_of_component are the same one-line argument on the other two projections.\n\nSame sort and kind, a different component: a distinct dedicated kind. The water content and the air content of soil share their sort of system and their kind-of-property, differing only in the component — and that alone makes them distinct dedicated kinds.\n\nCongruence on the component projection, exactly as for distinct_of_kind.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Dedicated kinds-of-property","header":"9.1. The dedicated kind (Dybkær Ch. 20)","id":"/Dedicated-kinds-of-property/The-dedicated-kind-_LPAR_Dybk___r-Ch___-20_RPAR_/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Dedicated-kinds-of-property--The-dedicated-kind-_LPAR_Dybk___r-Ch___-20_RPAR_"},"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L0-___-The-black-box/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L0-___-The-black-box--Synopsis--PKC-vocabulary-in-scope":{"contents":"New at this step:\n\n* Quantity — a magnitude of one fixed kind of property, with no bearer yet.* angularFrequencyDK — a dimensioned angular-frequency kind (.kind, .dim).* angularFrequency, frequency — two ISO 80000-3 kinds that share dimension T⁻¹.* massQ, springQ — read PhysLib's oscillator mass and spring constant as kinded quantities.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L0 — The black box\u0009Synopsis","header":"5.1.1.2. PKC vocabulary in scope","id":"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L0-___-The-black-box/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L0-___-The-black-box--Synopsis--PKC-vocabulary-in-scope"},"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L2-___-The-pair-is-a-system-too/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L2-___-The-pair-is-a-system-too--Learning-path":{"contents":"* #* Do* Checked by HCP.L2_ThePair anchor* Concept disclosed\n* 1* Open the boxes into bodies A/B and springs kA/kC/kB, give each part a nominal pair-indexed bearer, and compare compressed/stretched kC configurations of pair P.* HCP.L2_ThePair#opened_pair* Mass belongs to a body, stiffness and signed extension belong to a spring; extensionOf has a typed same-pair difference.\n* 2* Assemble both body masses into the pair whole.* HCP.L2_ThePair#pair_mass* The pair-specific whole mass is the sum of its parts' masses, read from the real fields.\n* 3* Try to add the pair mass to body A's mass and to a pair with identical parameters but a different PairId.* HCP.L2_ThePair#refused_whole_part* Whole and part are distinct bearers; P and Q are different systems even when all parameter values and part names match.\n* 4* Try to assemble the bodies' angular frequencies into the pair.* HCP.L2_ThePair#refused_frequency_assembly* Angular frequency has no Assembles license for the pair sort.\n* 5* Give the normal mode its dedicated kind at the pair sort.* HCP.L2_ThePair#normal_mode* The normal mode is neither a body nor a spring property.\n* 6* With B clamped, assemble kA and kC as springs in parallel.* HCP.L2_ThePair#parallel_stiffness* The parallel whole has the sum of the two stiffness magnitudes.\n* 7* Try to assemble kA and kB as two springs chained end to end.* HCP.L2_ThePair#refused_series_stiffness* No series-sort assembly license is registered.\n* 8* Check the aggregation licenses on the exhibited pairs: parallel kA–kC and series kA–kB.* HCP.L2_ThePair#parallelLaw; HCP.L2_ThePair#seriesLaw; HCP.L2_ThePair#licenseCashed* Parallel stiffness adds; series kA–kB gives 6 ∥ 3 = 2. Integer measurements are exact only for the exhibited pairs and do not model longer chains.\n\nWorksheet: HCP/Worksheets/L2_ThePair.lean · Solution: HCP/L2_ThePair.lean\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L2 — The pair is a system too","header":"5.3.6. Learning path","id":"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L2-___-The-pair-is-a-system-too/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L2-___-The-pair-is-a-system-too--Learning-path"},"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L6-___-What-must-hold/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L6-___-What-must-hold--Learning-path":{"contents":"* #* Do* Checked by HCP.L6_Requirements anchor* Concept disclosed\n* 1* Assemble the two body masses into a pair mass and state R1 over pairS.* r1_pair_mass* The theorem reads the pair's own body quantities; the executable check evaluates 3 + 5 = 8.\n* 2* State R2 as the absence of the pair-sort assembly license for normal-mode frequency.* r2_no_aggregation* A whole-only frequency does not inherit the body-mass aggregation license.\n* 3* Scope the coupling-spring rating to P and compare two P records against a marked datasheet limit.* r3_empirical* The empirical requirement is gated by a Bool that names the referent and is spot-checked on two states of P.\n* 4* Replace P with a bare real, then replace pairS with a declaration that is not a sort.* invalid_scopes* Object scope requires a Sorted instance; sort scope requires a SortOfSystem.\n* 5* Survey requirements, census every produced model port, and run the clean gate.* requirement_census* Every produced port in these boundaries is governed; the invalid-scope twins are pinned counterexamples.\n\nWorksheet: HCP/Worksheets/L6_Requirements.lean · Solution: HCP/L6_Requirements.lean\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L6 — What must hold","header":"5.7.6. Learning path","id":"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L6-___-What-must-hold/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L6-___-What-must-hold--Learning-path"},"/Uncertainty-quantification-and-numerical-adequacy/Two-orthogonal-axes___-and-why-their-properties-compose/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Uncertainty-quantification-and-numerical-adequacy--Two-orthogonal-axes___-and-why-their-properties-compose":{"contents":"A science model is written once as a carrier-polymorphic kernel over [NumCarrier α] (the WO1\ndiscipline of the previous chapter). On top of that single source sit two orthogonal\nparametrization axes:\n\n* the numeric carrier  (Axis N) — how the numbers are represented: ℝ for\n  proof, an autograd tape for sensitivity, FP32/RInterval for numerical adequacy, CudaT/\n  Tensor for GPU execution, Float for a fast CPU reference;* the uncertainty descriptor (Axis U) — what is known about an input's dispersion: a\n  per-quantity distribution carrying its moments, an inverse cumulative distribution function,\n  and a support range.\n\nThe value of parametrizing both is that the guarantees compose — the same model yields a\nproof, a sensitivity, a numerical-adequacy certificate, and a GPU run as different\ninterpretations, not as four re-implementations:\n\n* Proof carrier (ℝ). The three uncertainty methods below are one provably nested\n  ladder; numerical adequacy is a theorem, not a heuristic.* the autograd tape (sensitivity). Exact sensitivity coefficients \n  for the linearized methods, and local surrogates for the sampling method.* the uncertainty descriptor (noise). Measurement uncertainty becomes a first-class,\n  composable property of a quantity, in the spirit of the VIM.* FP32/RInterval (no information loss). A certificate that the model does not silently drop\n  a small-but-significant input uncertainty — at the scale that the same descriptor defines.* Float/CudaT (fast execution). The identical model runs, batched, on CPU and GPU.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Uncertainty quantification and numerical adequacy","header":"19.2. Two orthogonal axes, and why their properties compose","id":"/Uncertainty-quantification-and-numerical-adequacy/Two-orthogonal-axes___-and-why-their-properties-compose/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Uncertainty-quantification-and-numerical-adequacy--Two-orthogonal-axes___-and-why-their-properties-compose"}});